# Line endings are pinned so a clone behaves the same on every machine.
# With only the system-wide `core.autocrlf=true` in play, a Linux or macOS
# clone would get LF batch files, and a Windows clone would get CRLF sources
# that differ from every other checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe is unreliable with bare LF inside `if`/`for` blocks, and run.bat is
# the documented way to launch the tool.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# The captured fixture is evidence, not source: keep its bytes exactly as
# extracted from the editor so the calibration figures stay reproducible.
*.epru -text

# Binary assets.
#
# ⚠️ **Every binary type must be listed here.** The catch-all above is
# ``text=auto eol=lf``, so an unlisted binary is one heuristic away from being
# line-ending-normalised on checkout. Git currently detects ``face.onnx`` as
# binary because it happens to contain NUL bytes early, but that is luck, not a
# guarantee -- and a corrupted model fails at run time rather than at clone
# time, which is the worst place to find out.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.bmp binary
*.onnx binary
*.bin binary
*.pth binary
*.pt binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
